course: dictionary
lesson: 6829
title: One little word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word out is small, but it has many meanings. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning fits. Ask yourself: is something moving away, is it finished, or is it being seen?
explain: «Badger: «a stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge.» Here out means away from the inside of something, because the head came from inside the hedge. Check by swapping in another word: peered from a hedge makes sense too, so out tells you the direction.
explain: Out can also mean used up or not working. «They ran out of milk» means there is no milk left. «The light is out» means the light is not working. Out can mean not at home too, as in «Dad is out».
explain: Out is also a casual word. «I'm out» means I'm leaving, and «out of it» means tired or not paying attention. The tone of the sentence and the words next to out often show you which meaning is right.
fact: Out means away from the inside of something, as in «peered out of a hedge».
fact: Out can mean used up or finished, as in «ran out» or «the light is out».
fact: Out can mean not at home, or no longer in play in a game.
fact: «Out of» often names a total: «18 out of 20» means 18 from a group of 20.
fact: Casual talk uses out too: «I'm out» means I'm leaving, and «out of it» means tired or not paying attention.
exercise: «Badger: «a stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge.» What does out mean here? | *Away from the inside of something | Not at home | Seen or known | Finished or used up
exercise: «The children ran out of ideas by lunchtime.» What does out mean here? | Not working | *Used up or finished | Away from the inside of something | Seen by everyone
exercise: In «18 correct responses out of a possible 20», what does out of show? | Someone leaving the room | A game that is no longer in play | The number 18 is bigger than 20 | *A total: 18 of the 20 possible
test: Jayden says, «Dad is out, so let's wait.» What does out mean? | Seen or known | Used up | Away from the inside of something | *Not at home
test: In casual talk, what does «I'm out» mean? | Dancing very fast | I'm angry with you | *I'm leaving or not joining | I'm very hungry
exam: What does «the supply was exhausted» mean? | Full to the brim | Very loud | *Used up | Lost in the woods
